.TH RPULLOUT 1 "September 2026" "rpullout 2.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
rpullout \- pull the last matching substring before a regex, out of a stream

.SH SYNOPSIS
.B rpullout
[\-d] [\-m]
.I want amatch

.SH DESCRIPTION
.B rpullout
is like
.BR pullout (1),
but anchored from the end instead of the start: it has no
.I bmatch
argument. For each match of
.RI ' want ... amatch '
it prints the
.B last
(rightmost) occurrence of
.I want
that appears before
.IR amatch ", "
not simply the first one a lazy regex search would find. This is useful when you know what comes
.I after
the value you want but nothing reliable comes before it.

Without \-m, each input line is matched independently and only the first (outermost) match per line is
used. With \-m, the whole input is read at once, "." is allowed to match a newline, and every
non-overlapping match is reported. Matching is always case-insensitive.

.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-d
Do not insert the default ".*?" gap between
.I want
and
.I amatch
in the initial search; require them to be adjacent. (The internal scan that finds the
.B last
occurrence of
.I want
within the gap is unaffected by this flag.)
.TP
.B \-m
Multiline mode: read all of stdin at once, let "." match newlines, and report every match instead of one
per line.

.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
.I want
The regex for the substring you want printed. Must not be empty.
.TP
.I amatch
A regex that must occur after
.IR want .
Must not be empty.

.SH EXAMPLES
.RS
.B echo 'one two three STOP' | rpullout '\ew+' 'STOP'
.br
three
.RE

.RS
.B cat /etc/hosts | rpullout '^\ed\eS+' 'cmdesktop'
.RE

.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success (including "no lines matched").
.TP
.B 1
.I want
or
.I amatch
was empty.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error (missing arguments).

.SH AUTHOR
Chris Pollitt.

.SH SEE ALSO
.BR pullout (1),
.BR grep (1),
.BR re (7)
